\description{
Estimates average treatment effects on the treated (ATT) using either the
Callaway-Sant'Anna (2021) or Borusyak-Jaravel-Spiess (2024) estimator,
aggregated to a single summary effect (\code{"simple"}), per-cohort effects
(\code{"by_cohort"}), or per calendar-time effects (\code{"by_time"}).
}
\details{
This function complements \code{\link[=event_study]{event_study()}}: use \code{event_study()} when you
want a full event-study curve (dynamic effects by relative time), and
\code{att()} when you want aggregated ATT estimates that collapse the time
dimension. Both take the same formula, and treatment is given in the same
three ways (see \code{\link[=event_study]{event_study()}}, \strong{Specifying treatment}):

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{att(y ~ D | id + year, data = df, time = year)                 # D column
att(y ~ 1 | id + year, data = df, time = year, timing = g)     # timing column
att(y ~ treated | id + year, data = df, time = year, timing = 2005)
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

The result has \code{broom::tidy()} and \code{broom::glance()} methods, so it can be
passed to \code{modelsummary::modelsummary()} (rendered with \code{tinytable}) to build
a publication table — including stacking several estimators (e.g. \code{\link[=did]{did()}}
TWFE, CS, and BJS) side by side in one table.

\code{att()} supersedes \code{\link[=calc_att]{calc_att()}} as of fixes 1.0.0.
}
\section{Aggregation formulas (CS estimator)}{

\itemize{
\item \strong{simple}: \eqn{\theta = \sum_g (n_g/n_{treated}) \cdot \overline{ATT(g,\cdot)}}
where \eqn{\overline{ATT(g,\cdot)}} is the mean over post-treatment periods.
\item \strong{by_cohort}: \eqn{\theta(g) = \overline{ATT(g,\cdot)}} per cohort.
\item \strong{by_time}: \eqn{\theta(t) = \sum_{g \le t} w(g,t) \cdot ATT(g,t)}
with \eqn{w(g,t) = n_g / \sum_{g' \le t} n_{g'}}.
}

Rows with a missing or non-finite outcome, time, or unit are dropped and
the panel is balanced by dropping units not observed in every period, as
\code{\link[did:att_gt]{did::att_gt()}} does by default; both steps warn, and \code{N}, \code{N_units},
\code{N_treated} and \code{N_nevertreated} describe the balanced sample.
}

\section{Standard errors (BJS estimator)}{

BJS SEs are approximate (naive sample variance of unit-time effects).
Cluster-robust SEs for BJS aggregations are planned for a future release.
}

